= Notes on Growth and Angles

A square of side $x$ has area $x^2$, so doubling the side makes the area four times larger. When two angles $alpha + beta$ add to a right angle, each one is the complement of the other.

The share of a whole that one part takes is the fraction $a/b$, and it never exceeds one while $a <= b$. A rate that halves each step falls as $2^(-n)$ after $n$ steps.

== The quadratic formula

Every equation of the form $a x^2 + b x + c = 0$ with $a != 0$ has the roots below.

$ x = (-b plus.minus sqrt(b^2 - 4 a c)) / (2 a) $

The sign under the root, $b^2 - 4 a c$, tells how many real roots there are.

